arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini: declare the DA9214's two CPU rails

Which of the DA9214's bucks is which is now a MEASUREMENT, which is exactly
what enabling this bus (dts/0041) was for. Read from the chip on #46, once
i2c/0001 made its data phase work:

  BUCKA_CONT 0x5D = 0x01   ENABLED,  VBUCKA_A 0xD7 = 0x46 -> 1000 mV
  BUCKB_CONT 0x5E = 0x00   DISABLED, VBUCKB_A 0xD9 = 0x46 -> 1000 mV

BUCKA is the live CPU rail, and its 1000 mV is exactly the figure `vproc_fixed`
asserts as a regulator-fixed fiction — so the A53 clusters run off BUCKA, not
off the RT5735 on i2c7 that B-43 supposed. BUCKB is VPROC2, the Cortex-A72
cluster's supply, and it has never been switched on.

Two facts about identity, both measured rather than assumed:

 - DEVICE_ID (mainline's 0x201 = PAGE_CON 0x04, register 0x01) reads 0x24,
   which mainline calls DA9215 and accepts. The vendor names the part DA9214
   and binds its own da9214 driver to it under Android. Both are true: the
   compatible names the part, and the driver takes chip_id from the silicon.
 - mainline's paged regmap shifts the page by one (PAGE_CON = page << 1) where
   the vendor writes the raw field value. Mainline's is the encoding that
   reads a sane DEVICE_ID here, so its regmap is right and the vendor's
   convention is simply different.

BUCKA is pinned min == max and always-on deliberately. This node exists so the
rail can be OBSERVED through the regulator framework; moving it is cpufreq's
job, and cpufreq cannot bind yet because mt6797's clock driver models no
ARMPLL and the CPU clocks therefore do not exist. Until that lands, nothing
should be able to change the voltage the running CPUs depend on. Widen the
range in the same change that adds the OPP tables, not before.

BUCKB is declared and deliberately not always-on. It is off, and B-40 has since
shown that turning it on does not bring the A72s up — ATF stalls in the MP2
power sequence with the rail up or down.

CONFIG_REGULATOR_DA9211 stays =m so nothing binds until `modprobe da9211`,
after the disable-unused sweep has already run.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -805,6 +805,71 @@
 	 */
 	mediatek,appm_used;
 	status = "okay";
+
+	/*
+	 * The DA9214 dual buck: the two CPU core rails, identified by
+	 * MEASUREMENT rather than inference, which is what enabling this bus
+	 * (dts/0041) was for.
+	 *
+	 * Read from the chip on #46, once i2c/0001 made its data phase work:
+	 *
+	 *   BUCKA_CONT 0x5D = 0x01   ENABLED,  VBUCKA_A 0xD7 = 0x46 -> 1000 mV
+	 *   BUCKB_CONT 0x5E = 0x00   DISABLED, VBUCKB_A 0xD9 = 0x46 -> 1000 mV
+	 *
+	 * BUCKA is the live CPU rail. Its 1000 mV is exactly the figure
+	 * `vproc_fixed` above asserts as a regulator-fixed fiction, so the A53
+	 * clusters run off BUCKA — not off the RT5735 on i2c7, which B-43 had
+	 * supposed. BUCKB is VPROC2, the Cortex-A72 cluster's supply, and it
+	 * has never been switched on (B-40).
+	 *
+	 * Two notes on identity, both measured:
+	 *
+	 *  - DEVICE_ID (mainline's 0x201 = PAGE_CON 0x04, register 0x01) reads
+	 *    0x24, which mainline calls DA9215 and accepts. The vendor names
+	 *    the part DA9214 and binds its own `da9214` driver to it under
+	 *    Android. Both are true: `dlg,da9214` is the part, and the driver
+	 *    takes its chip_id from the silicon, not from this compatible.
+	 *  - mainline's paged regmap shifts the page by one (PAGE_CON = page
+	 *    << 1) where the vendor writes the raw field. Mainline's is the
+	 *    one that reads a sane DEVICE_ID here, so its regmap is right.
+	 *
+	 * WHY BUCKA IS PINNED AND ALWAYS-ON. This node exists so the rail can
+	 * be *observed* through the regulator framework; moving it is cpufreq's
+	 * job and cpufreq cannot bind yet (mt6797's clock driver models no
+	 * ARMPLL, so the CPU clocks do not exist). Until then min == max makes
+	 * it impossible for anything to change the voltage the running CPUs
+	 * depend on, and regulator-always-on keeps the disable-unused sweep
+	 * away from it. Widen the range in the same change that lands the OPP
+	 * tables, not before.
+	 *
+	 * BUCKB is declared and deliberately NOT always-on: it is off, and
+	 * B-40 has now shown that turning it on does not bring the A72s up —
+	 * ATF stalls in the MP2 power sequence regardless.
+	 *
+	 * CONFIG_REGULATOR_DA9211 stays =m (configs/gemini-cpu-regulator.config)
+	 * so nothing binds until `modprobe da9211`, after the disable-unused
+	 * sweep has already run.
+	 */
+	da9214: regulator@68 {
+		compatible = "dlg,da9214";
+		reg = <0x68>;
+
+		regulators {
+			da9214_vproc: BUCKA {
+				regulator-name = "da9214_vproc";
+				regulator-min-microvolt = <1000000>;
+				regulator-max-microvolt = <1000000>;
+				regulator-always-on;
+				regulator-boot-on;
+			};
+
+			da9214_vproc2: BUCKB {
+				regulator-name = "da9214_vproc2";
+				regulator-min-microvolt = <800000>;
+				regulator-max-microvolt = <1180000>;
+			};
+		};
+	};
 };
 
